it's always good to know who's doing the  digging so in this section I will talk  about Jorge Andrew Reisner short  biographical sketch Reisner was born in  1867 in Indianapolis that makes him a  Hoosier he came to Harvard in the class  of 1889 and in the last few years of his  studies he became interested in Semitic  languages ancient cultures of the  ancient Near East he'd first heard about  this in some of his high school classes  back in Indianapolis he graduated in the  class of 1889 and you see a class  photograph here in this picture along  the way he studied under David Gordon  Lyon Lyon was the one who founded the  Harvard Semitic museum got this building  built in 1903 that you see here and he  was really the father of what is now the  Department of Near Eastern languages and  civilizations Reisner went home to  Indianapolis after getting his BA degree  and worked in a law firm and then he  came back to Harvard for his master's  degree in 1891 and his PhD in 1893 along  the way he took time out to go back home  and marry the girl next door  Mary Bronson David Gordon Lyon had  studied in Leipzig and he felt it was  important for Eisner to have the  experience of a German academic  institution so he sent him off to Berlin  and it's there that Reisner began his  Egyptological career remember at Harvard  it was all about Semitic languages so  Biblical Hebrew Akkadian cuneiform  inscriptions and the like it was at  Berlin that he met the great  Egyptologist Adolph airmen who had  studied so many phases of the Egyptian  language and his star pupil Court Zeta  and in the Berlin Museum Reisner learned  Egyptian all phases of the language from  Zeta he thought everything was set after  spending three years at Berlin and he  came back to Harvard and taught for a  year but to his great surprise there was  no money to keep him and so all seemed  lost until he got a letter from Berlin  from one of his colleagues Ludwig  Borchardt  inviting him to come to Cairo and be  part of an international commission to  study and publish objects in the  chipton museum this was his first  good-paying job and of course he jumped  at it so here is a photograph of the  International Commission team members  from several different countries all  working on different aspects of the  Egyptian Museum collection it was here  in Cairo that Reisner met Phoebe  Apperson Hearst mother of the newspaper  magnate William Randolph Hearst she was  looking for excavators to support  projects all over the world in Egypt too  for the University of California  Berkeley  so in 1899 Hearst and Reisner signed an  agreement and he decided to change  directions completely from Semitic  philology to Egyptian object publishing  and become an archaeologist this was a  whole right turn for him and the  agreement called for him basically to  learn on the job  not a bad understanding if you can get  it and so he started in Upper Egypt  southern parts of the country working at  quote-unquote less important sites in  order to hone his craft inventing survey  techniques and record-keeping for his  excavations this map shows you some of  the early sites that he worked at and  there's a photograph of the camp at Naga  Adair which came to be a very important  site ultimately but it was later on in  1903 that the Giza necropolis became  available there had been just too much  illicit digging and Reisner was not the  only one complaining to the Egyptian  Antiquities authorities then of course  under French control under Gaston  Maspero Maspero allowed several  different expeditions to come together  and Amelie divided up the site and this  is what happened so in this section I'm  just going to talk about reissner's  excavations in another module you've  seen how the site was divided between  the German later German Austrian the  Italian and the American concessions  this plan shows the dates in red type  for how Reisner moved all across the  necropolis he began in 1904 at the far  western edge of the site and moved  gradually eastwards towards the Great  Pyramid along the way he picked up a  student from Harvard Albert Morton  Lithgow who had transferred from Brown  to come to Harvard and had been trained  at the American School in Athens and  elsewhere Lithgow would later leave to  become the first curator at the  Metropolitan Museum of Art  everything was going just fine until  Phoebe Hearst ran into financial trouble  and she needed to pull out from  supporting the excavation this was in  1904 1905 and this was a catastrophe  so in the summer of 1905 Lithgow came  back to Harvard first and Reisner  followed later and the idea was to try  to get the Museum of Fine Arts Boston  and/or Harvard University interested in  picking up the excavation so this began  what I call the intriguing summer of  1905 and this photograph shows you many  of the players we have Harvard's  president Eliot looking after Harvard's  interest David Gordon Lyon reissner's  old professor trying to do right by  everyone Albert mortlake Oh reissner's  supporter and assistant but really had  his own agenda along the way as well and  then on the museum side gardner martin  lane was representing the interests of  the museum so there was a lot of back  and forth and eventually was created the  Harvard University Boston Museum of Fine  Arts expedition here's a photograph of  the old Copley Square building for the  Museum of Fine Arts in those days in  1909 the building moved to Huntington  Avenue the site that it still occupies  today this was a one-year contract and  was meant to expire in 1906 but it  continued as a sort of gentlemen's  agreement between these institutions for  the next 40 years all the way until  reissner's death in 1942 and so the  object started flowing in here was a  photograph of the MFA galleries and you  see some of the Giza masterpieces set up  in the room crates were arriving records  photographed notes publications Reisner  was writing articles in the MFA bulletin  and elsewhere and all of this took place  from the headquarters at what was known  as Harvard camp here you see it on this  aerial photograph the red arrow points  to the location to the west of khafre's  pyramid this was just a simple  collection of mud brick huts but it  eventually grew over the years it had  the engineers offices the photo studios  reissner's office a dining area and of  course sleeping quarters this photograph  shows you the courtyard which was always  choked with objects fragments of pottery  stone vessels and from here a lot of the  colossal statues came together there's  also a computer Maude  we've made of this area and it's  important to remember that Reisner was  not only famous for developing  archaeological method and these great  documentation systems at the Giza  pyramids but all up and down the Nile  here is a photograph of him working all  the way down south in Nubia he was  basically the father of Nubian  archeology as well  this image shows one of the class  notebooks the class of 1889 and you can  see how Reisner is aging eventually by  the 1930s he was the grey eminence he  was the sage up at Harvard camp and  everyone would come to consult him not  only about matters archaeological but  political issues as well remember you  have the British running the government  you have the French controlling the  antiquities service and the Egyptians of  course desiring their own independence  along the way so this photograph from an  Arabic newspaper shows him an advanced  age unfortunately he's losing his sight  by this time but again he was the master  the expert whom so many came to consult  and I couldn't help noticing recently on  the internet this photograph of some  mirrored sunglasses that show the  pyramids reflecting and I got a chuckle  because Reisner had done this already  before we can compare this Internet  image to the photograph taken from that  Arabic newspaper and you can see the  pyramid reflecting in reissner's glasses  as well there was even a live broadcast  the first time ever made by NBC right  from the pyramids on a cold winter  evening in February 1938 Reisner and the  team assembled on the North Face of the  Khufu pyramid and they broadcast live  this would have been dinner time back  home in the US but it was about 11:00  p.m. in Cairo and he narrated some of  the excavation work loads and some of  his assistant spoke as well and a famous  radio personality inside the Kings  chamber inside Khufu also broadcast this  required miles of cable going down to  the Mina House Hotel off the edge of the  plateau and then briefly just some of  his trusted assistants there was dows  Dunham a Harvard student who had come  out to Giza and became Rhys doors  right-hand man also working in Nubia and  then eventually coming back to the  Museum of Fine Arts being in all but  name the chief curator as all the crates  and the objects and the monuments came  in there was another Harvard student  named Willie  Stevenson Smith he too eventually became  a successor and curator in the Egyptian  Department after reissner's death great  art historian who published many  important works about the Old Kingdom  and the pyramid age and then Reisner  befriended a couple Joseph Linden Smith  and his wife Karina Smith was a painter  who'd studied at the school of the  Museum of Fine Arts and elsewhere  turning from portrait painting he found  his true love coming out to Egypt every  year and painting scenes in Giza tombs  temples Nubia Egypt everywhere so he  became great friends with Reisner and  they enjoyed their company very much  reisender was not such a great fan of  the burgeoning color photography process  but he thought the oil paintings made by  Smith were tremendously valuable and  there are well over a hundred of them  today and then finally we have syed  ahmed Siyad one of his fifties these are  the egyptian foremen from a town in  upper egypt who ran the show and messiah  Ahmed was whenever eisenerz most trusted  individuals took him on as a  photographic helper and a boy and he  spent decades with the expedition  unfortunately he died in 1926 during the  excavation of the head of Harris tomb  but side sons Muhammad and Mahmoud went  on to follow in this tradition and they  ran everything at the expedition after  that keeping the accounts keeping the  men in line doing the pay stubs for  everyone one of them learned English and  was typing up a lot of the manuscripts  as well Reisner was only able to come  back to America four or five times to  teach at Harvard remember World War one  got in the way and delayed things and  his very last trip was in 1939 when he  got an honorary degree of the Harvard  commencement this was his 50th  commencement anniversary after that he  returned to his beloved Harvard camp  remember he's pretty much blind by this  time World War two breaks out he sends  his wife and daughter home to America  they had quite a circuitous route but  they finally made it and in 1942  unfortunately after a long stroke and  basically being bedridden Reisner died  at Harvard camp behind the pyramids  he's buried in the old part of Cairo in  the American Cemetery and his men and  the Museum of Fine Arts team composed  this text that you see on the screen  here and this is the inscription that  you read when you visit his tombstone  today back at Giza  you can still see vestiges of the great  era of excavations some of these rusting  debris carts and even railroad tracks  some of them still used today that were  used to move the large mounds off of the  mastaba tombs during all those decades  of excavation  you
 

